  "strategy": "1. Approach and rationale\nThe central idea is to optimize the step function using a genetic algorithm combined with a Bayesian optimization layer to escape local optima. Unlike the provided parent construction, we use a population-based approach that explores diverse solutions while maintaining the bipartite structure of the step function. We address the non-convex, high-dimensional nature of the problem by leveraging:\n\n- Soft constraint handling to avoid projections (which can be computationally expensive)\n- Bayesian optimization with expected improvement acquisition function to balance exploration and exploitation\n- Elitism to retain the best solutions found during optimization\n- Dynamic parameter settings for mutation and crossover to adapt to terrain ruggedness\n- A hybrid of Gaussian process-based Bayesian optimization and stochastic gradient descent\n\nThis approach is expected to outperform the parent solution by systematically exploring the function space while respecting optimization constraints through probabilistic relaxation. The main limitation is the relatively high-dimensional space (200-dimensional simplex), which requires careful handling of the stochasticity in the optimization process.\n\n2. Mathematical specification\nWe represent the step function h(x): [0,2] \u2192 [0,1] as an n-dimensional vector (n=200) satisfying:\n- Sum(h_i) * dx = 1 (integral constraint)\n- 0 \u2264 h_i \u2264 1 for all i\n\nThe objective is to minimize:\nC5 = max_{k} \u222b h(x) * (1 - h(x+k)) dx = dx * max_{k} |correlation_full(h, 1-h)[k]|\n\nThe optimization occurs in the space of constrained vectors. Key components:\n- Bayesian optimization layer uses Gaussian process with Mat\u00e9rn kernel\n- Acquisition function is expected improvement (EI)\n- The optimization is formulated as:\n  min_h f(h) \n  Subject to: \u2211h_i dx = 1, 0 \u2264 h_i \u2264 1\n- Solutions are decoded as step functions through:\n  h(x) = (n/2) / (L^2) * [sin(\u03c0*x/L)\u039b(x/L)]   (example smooth interpolation)\n\n3. Implementation procedure\nStep-by-step execution:\n\nA Input handling (assume n=200, dx=0.01, target_sum=100.0 if parent provided)\n\nB Initialization:\n1. Load initial_h_values (200-point parent solution if available)\n2. Perform L-BFGS optimization from parent (seeded)\n3. Generate local population via mutation\n4. Project parent and random samples to feasible set (0-1 and integral=100)\n- Projection: minimize \u03b5 subject to ||x - proj|| \u2264 \u03b5, \u2211proj_i dx=1, 0\u2264proj_i\u22641\n\nC Optimization loop:\n1. Gaussian process regression on population members\n2. Expected improvement acquisition function evaluation\n3. Stochastic gradient descent with adaptive learning rate\n4. Cross-entropy based Bayesian updates\n5. Diversity control via tournament selection (effective range [0.5,1.5])\n\nD Projection and constraint-handling:\n1. (Pseudo-code) handle_inequalities(x):\n   - Scale features to [0,1]\n   - Apply entropy regularizer\n   - Iteratively set bounds with projection\n\nE Post-optimization:\n1. Convert winning vector to step function\n2. Re-validate using central difference approximation (for robustness)\n\nF Fallback: If timed out, return uniform grid solution with projected integral\n\n4. Search choices and useful variations\n- Primary: L-BFGS with damping (initial phase)\n- Secondary: Bayesian adaptive Monte Carlo (after initial phase)\n- Tertiary: Stochastic gradient descent (clipped) - 0.1 initial rate, 0.999 decay\n\n5. Compute budget and robustness\nDominant costs: \n- Correlation evaluation: O(200^2) ~ 40,000 operations\n- Projection: O(200) per vector\n- Acquisition function: O(n^2)\n\nTermination triggers at 100 evaluations or 1500 seconds (allowing 15 evaluations/second). \nFallback available if more than 5 consecutive iterations show no improvement.\n\n6. Validation and return contract\n- Check: sum(h) * dx=1 (allow 0.5% relative error)\n- Compute C5 via correlation over 10,000 random shifts (robustness check)\n- Re-scale vector to [0,2] domain\n- Fallback: if parent unavailable, local solution around (0.5)\n- Return format: (h_values, c5_bound, n_points)",
